ants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/255981"} "72 from 18 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "Hungary, the defending Olympic champion in the K-4 1000, had not lost this event at the World Championships since 1985 and its lineup for the 1992 Summer Olympics, " [:a {:href "/athletes/10054"} "László Fidel"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/10048"} "Ferenc Csipes"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/10061"} "Zsolt Gyulay"] ", and " [:a {:href "/athletes/10036"} "Attila Ábrahám"] ", were all seasoned veterans. They won their opening heat with ease, setting an Olympic record in the process, while Australia and Germany took the others. East Germany had taken bronze in the event at the 1989 and 1990 World Championships, while the unified team had been runner-up in 1991 and brought three members of that squad, " [:a {:href "/athletes/9936"} "Oliver Kegel"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/9966"} "Thomas Reineck"] ", and " [:a {:href "/athletes/10000"} "André Wohllebe"] ", augmented by " [:a {:href "/athletes/9993"} "Mario von Appen"] ". The Germans then proceeded to defeat the Hungarians in the semi-finals by a margin of 0.59 seconds, while Romania took the other heat. The Germans maintained their strength into the final, beating the Hungarians to the gold medal by 0.64 seconds and leaving the reigning Olympic and World Champions with silver. Australia was over two seconds behind, but still managed to steal gold from the Czechoslovakians by 0.09 seconds."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitors"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "–"] [:td {} "Germany"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GER"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GER.png"}] "GER"]] [:td {} "2:52.17 (1 h3)"] [:td {} "2:54.80 (1 h1)"] 